Areas the tiles do not cover yet show the background, not stale or undefined
pixels, and the tiles ahead of a scroll come sooner.

Tiles are created and recorded by the main thread; the scrolling thread can
only show those that exist.  When it scrolls past them -- the main thread
busy for longer than it takes to scroll through the cover area, half a
viewport ahead -- the uncovered part of the view has to be filled with
something, and three things decide what and for how long:

  - What.  AcceleratedSurface cleared the viewport with an opaque background
    colour only for frames the scrolling thread asked for, on the grounds
    that a rendering update covers the viewport with tiles.  With threaded
    scrolling it need not: the frame of a rendering update is composited with
    the scrolling tree's layer positions, which may have moved past what that
    update created tiles for.  Those pixels kept whatever the buffer held --
    an older frame at another offset, or memory nothing wrote, which is grey
    on VMware (its undefined surface contents are 0xcc) -- until the tiles
    arrived, seconds later on a slow main thread.  Every frame is now
    cleared; one clear is a small part of compositing a frame.

  - Which tiles first.  createOrDestroyTiles() collects the missing tiles
    closest to the viewport, but on finding a closer one it cleared the
    wrong vector (the output, which is empty there, instead of the positions
    collected so far), so tiles above the viewport, visited first, were
    created and painted along with the visible ones, ahead of them.  Same
    change as WebKit 322955@main (bug 326498).

  - How far ahead.  The cover rect reaches half a viewport beyond the
    visible rect on every side.  It now reaches as far again in the
    direction the visible rect last moved, so that the main thread, each
    time it gets to run, prepares a full viewport of the page ahead of the
    scroll.  Those tiles are the farthest from the viewport and are created
    last; under memory pressure, where the cover area shrinks to the
    viewport, nothing changes.

